Good—this is exactly the kind of preparation that helps your lawyer use the status conference effectively. Below I’ve put together: (A) focused questions to ask Robert Kim, grouped by topic; (B) a prioritized evidence checklist you can start collecting now; and (C) an 8-week action timeline so you’ll be ready for the status conference and next steps. Use or adapt any of these as an agenda when you meet or email him.

A. Key questions to ask your attorney (Robert Kim)
Case status and goals
- How does the civil division in Hennepin County typically use the status conference? What will the judge likely ask or expect from us?
- What immediate procedural deadlines or filings (proposed scheduling order, discovery plan, protective order, etc.) should we be prepared to propose at the conference?
- Given the mix of NDA dispute plus custody/support issues, are these matters consolidated in one proceeding or should any parts be handled in family court? If separate, how does that affect evidence/discovery?

Evidence & discovery
- What specific evidence do you need from me before the status conference to preserve or present to the court?
- Which documents/communications are highest priority (NDA, emails, texts, business records, financials, etc.)?
- Should we send a formal preservation letter to the opposing party now to prevent spoliation? If so, can you draft and send it?
- What discovery tools will we use first (requests for production, interrogatories, subpoenas, depositions)? Which should I start preparing for now?
- Are there any immediate steps we should take to preserve electronic evidence (back up devices, create forensic images, preserve metadata)?

Witnesses & experts
- Do we need any expert(s) (forensic IT, forensic accountant, custody evaluator) before the status conference or shortly after? What is typical timing and cost?
- Who are the likely fact witnesses we should identify now (employees, clients, third parties, childcare providers)?
- Should we start getting affidavits or written witness declarations now?

NDA-specific questions
- What elements of an NDA breach do we need to prove for the relief we want (breach, damages, confidentiality scope, misuse of confidential info)?
- What proof will the court expect for trade-secret or confidential information claims (evidence of measures to protect secrecy, list of secret items)?
- Do we need immediate injunctive relief or a temporary restraining order to prevent ongoing disclosure/use? If so, what evidence is required?

Custody & support specifics
- How will custody/support evidence be handled in this civil case? Should we simultaneously pursue family-court filings?
- What documents prove income and expenses for child support (pay stubs, tax returns, bank statements, child-care receipts)?
- What records show parenting time, parenting concerns, or the children’s needs (school records, medical, photographs, calendars, communications)?

Procedural & strategy
- What is our proposed case plan to present at the status conference (discovery schedule, mediation/ADR timeline, estimated trial date)?
- Do we need a protective/confidentiality order because we will disclose business documents or trade secrets during discovery?
- Are there settlement or ADR options we should prepare for before the conference?
- What are the anticipated costs and timeline for discovery and experts?

Immediate logistics
- What materials should I bring to our meeting? How should I organize them (electronic folders, exhibit binder, timelines)?
- Who at Kim & Associates will be handling day-to-day communications and filings?

B. Prioritized evidence checklist to assemble now
Top priority (preserve immediately)
- The signed NDA and any amendments or related agreements.
- All communications about the NDA and the alleged disclosures: emails, texts, DMs, Slack messages, LinkedIn messages—especially those involving your former partner or third parties.
- Any written notices, letters, or demand communications about the alleged breach.
- A clear chronology/timeline (dates of alleged breach, when you learned of it, actions you took).
- Backups: make copies of relevant devices/accounts, and note where originals are stored.

Business/financial proof (for damages & support overlap)
- Company documents: formation docs, bylaws, membership agreements, meeting minutes, client lists, vendor contracts.
- Financials: bank statements, invoices, receipts, profit & loss statements, payroll, tax returns.
- Evidence of loss or misuse (lost contracts, emails from clients, proof of diverted business, pricing lists).

Custody/support evidence
- Income proof: last 2–3 years of tax returns, pay stubs, bank statements.
- Parenting records: calendars, school attendance, medical records, daycare bills, photos, communications re scheduling/pickups.
- Any court orders or prior family-court filings relating to custody/support.

Technical and authentication evidence
- Source files, drafts, version history, metadata for documents claimed confidential.
- Logs, server records, access logs (if relevant), and any evidence showing who accessed or exported files.
- Preserve original devices (phones, laptops) or at least make forensic copies.

Witness material
- Names and contact info for potential witnesses: employees, clients, childcare providers, third parties who saw relevant conduct.
- Any witness statements you can obtain now (short signed or emailed statements).

Protective or sensitive material
- A proposed list of documents you consider confidential or trade secret (helps draft a protective order).
- Any NDAs signed between the business and clients which demonstrate confidentiality practices.

C. Suggested 8-week plan before the status conference (status conference is in ~8 weeks)
Week 1
- Meet with Robert Kim (or send him this checklist). Ask him to send a preservation letter and confirm if any emergency relief is needed.
- Start making secure backups of all relevant electronic devices/accounts.
- Create a master chronology of events (dates, actions, sources).

Weeks 2–3
- Collect key documents listed above and organize into folders (NDA, communications, financials, custody records).
- Identify possible witnesses and contact info.
- Ask Robert about experts; if recommended, get initial scoping and fee estimates.

Weeks 4–5
- Work with counsel to draft initial discovery requests and a proposed scheduling plan (if counsel will file these before/at conference).
- If needed, begin engaging experts (forensic IT or accountant) to secure quick preservation/analysis.

Weeks 6–7
- Prepare a short evidence memo for your attorney summarizing the strongest proof and gaps.
- Assemble an exhibit index and printed binder or organized secure electronic folder to share with counsel.

Week 8 (1–2 weeks before conference)
- Review and finalize proposed conference positions with Robert (proposed schedule, mediation request, discovery plan).
- Confirm who will attend the conference and what materials (proposed order, timeline) you’ll provide to the judge.

D. Sample short agenda/email you can send Robert
If you want, send your lawyer a short email like this:
- “Robert — I have a status conference in ~8 weeks. I’ve gathered the NDA, communications, and key financial and custody records. Can we meet this week to: (1) confirm what evidence you need before the status conference; (2) send a preservation letter to opposing counsel; (3) decide whether to retain any experts now; and (4) prepare a proposed scheduling/discovery plan for the judge? I’ve attached a preliminary timeline and list of documents I’ve collected. — [Your name]”

E. Final tips / things to watch for
- Preserve everything now. Courts take spoliation seriously; a preservation letter and immediate backups are low cost and high value.
- Don’t discuss case strategy with your former partner or post details online—keep communications through counsel where possible.
- Ask Robert about protective orders before producing sensitive business documents.
- Keep a running log of all communications and actions you take after you learn of the alleged breach.

If you’d like, I can:
- Turn the above into a one-page checklist you can print and bring to the meeting;
- Draft a preservation letter template for your attorney to review;
- Or draft the short email to Robert with the agenda and items to attach.

