High-level goal to tell your attorney
- Confirm whether you need additional evidence before the status conference (in two months) and, if so, what exactly is essential to collect and what can wait.

Questions to ask Robert Kim (grouped and ready to use)
1. Case status and schedule
- What is the precise purpose of the upcoming status conference (scheduling only, settlement discussion, case management)?
- What deadlines will the Court likely set at that conference (discovery cutoff, motion schedule, expert disclosure deadlines)?
- Are there any immediate filings you or I should prepare before the conference?

2. Evidence & discovery
- Based on the current pleading, what specific types of evidence do you think are necessary to prove the NDA breach and damages?
- Which documents are essential to have ready for initial disclosures or for the status conference itself?
- Are there specific emails, contracts, financial records, or other files you want me to prioritize collecting now?
- Should we serve discovery (interrogatories, requests for production) or a subpoena before the status conference? If so, what should they seek?

3. Preservation & chain-of-custody
- What immediate preservation steps should I take to ensure evidence isn’t lost (litigation hold, preserve devices, backup cloud accounts)?
- Do you recommend forensic imaging of devices or metadata preservation for emails/files? If yes, who will arrange it and how soon?

4. Privilege & NDA concerns
- Which communications are privileged and should not be produced or shared?
- Given the NDA, what can we disclose publicly or in court filings, and when should we seek a protective order or sealing?

5. Witnesses & experts
- Are there witnesses you want me to identify now (co-workers, customers, accountants)? How many and what facts should they testify about?
- Will we need experts (financial damages, forensic IT, valuation)? If so, when do we need to retain them and what will they require from me?

6. Custody & support interplay
- How will the custody and support issues affect the civil case (e.g., discovery scope, timing, confidentiality)? Do we need to coordinate with family-court counsel or consolidate information?
- Are there documents about custody/support (financial affidavits, payment records, parenting logs) that are relevant to damages or credibility in this civil matter?
- Should any custody/support evidence be handled differently to protect children’s privacy?

7. Motions & tactical questions
- Do you recommend any immediate motions (motion for temporary relief, motion to compel, protective order, preservation order)?
- What discovery or evidence gaps would make you consider filing a motion before the status conference?

8. Settlement & risk assessment
- What is our settlement posture now, and what evidence would strengthen our bargaining position?
- Are there particular pieces of evidence that could tilt the case toward settlement vs. proceeding?

9. Practical & administrative
- What should I bring to our next meeting with you? Do you want me to organize documents chronologically or by topic?
- How do you want me to communicate new evidence to you (email, secure client portal)?
- Are there local Hennepin County or Minnesota civil rules/forms we need to prepare for the status conference?

Essential documents and evidence checklist (prioritized)
A. NDA / contract evidence (highest priority)
- Original signed NDA(s), amendments, and any related contracts
- Dates NDA was signed and any dates of alleged disclosures

B. Evidence of disclosure / breach
- Emails, text messages, messaging app logs, Slack messages, DMs showing the alleged disclosure
- File transfer logs, cloud sharing links, or access logs
- Meeting notes, presentations, slide decks, or prototypes that were shared

C. Damages and mitigation proof
- Business financial records (profit/loss, balance sheets, tax returns) showing losses tied to the disclosure
- Sales records, lost contracts, client communications showing lost business
- Efforts to mitigate damages and related receipts or correspondence

D. Metadata and source files
- Original electronic files (not only PDFs or screenshots) with metadata when possible
- Email headers, server logs, timestamps

E. Custody & support documentation (if relevant)
- Income proof (pay stubs, tax returns) and payment histories for support
- Parenting time logs, communication about custody/exchanges, school or medical records relevant to disputes
- Any court orders in family court that may intersect

F. Communications & witnesses
- Chronological timeline of key events with supporting documents (one- or two-page timeline)
- List of potential witnesses with contact info and a short summary of what they can say
- Any third-party documents (clients, vendors) that corroborate events

Immediate preservation steps you should take now
- Do not delete any relevant messages or files. Preserve phones, computers, cloud accounts.
- Make backups of devices and email accounts (export PST/mbox or use a secure cloud backup).
- If other parties or third-parties hold data, let your lawyer know quickly about subpoenas or preservation letters.
- Keep a written log of any suspicious deletions or lost devices.

What the status conference often requires (so you can prepare)
- A brief case status update, proposed schedule, and identification of major issues (discovery, experts).
- The Court may set deadlines and conference dates rather than deciding substantive motions.
- You may not need to present full proof at the status conference, but you should be able to show the core documents exist and what discovery remains.

Suggested short checklist to bring to your meeting with Robert
- Chronological one-page timeline of key events
- Copies of the signed NDA and related contracts
- Top 10 documents you believe show the disclosure and top 5 documents showing damages
- List of witnesses and their expected testimony
- List of devices/accounts that might need preservation or forensic review
- Notes on any parallel family-court issues and relevant orders

Sample short script/email opener you can use to reach Robert
- “Robert — I want to be prepared for the status conference in two months. Can we review whether I need additional evidence before that date? I’ve prepared a timeline and have copies of the NDA, key emails, and financial records. Please let me know which items you want me to prioritize collecting and whether any immediate preservation steps or discovery requests are needed.”

Next steps I can help with
- Draft a concise email to Robert listing these questions and attaching your timeline and document list.
- Help you prioritize the documents you have into a “top 10” evidence list.
- Create a one-page timeline template you can fill in.
- Draft a preservation checklist/letter you can send to third parties (note: I can draft the text, but your lawyer should review before sending).

Would you like me to draft the email to Robert Kim now, or help you build the prioritized evidence list or timeline?

