Good — I can help you prepare a focused list to go over with Robert Kim so you’re ready for the status conference in two months. Below I’ve grouped concrete questions you can ask, plus a prioritized checklist of documents/evidence to gather, immediate preservation steps, and what to bring to your meeting. If you want, I can draft an email or a short message you can send to Robert with this list.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney (grouped and with short phrasing you can use)

A. Case strategy & status conference expectations
- What is the purpose of the status conference in civil division here, and what will the judge likely expect from us at that hearing?
- What do you want to accomplish by the status conference (deadlines, discovery plan, settlement conference, scheduling)?
- What deadlines will be set or come into effect after the status conference?

B. Evidence — what’s necessary and sufficient
- Based on our claims/defenses under the NDA, what specific evidence do we need to prove breach, damages, and causation?
- How strong is the evidence we already have? What are its weaknesses?
- Are there particular emails, texts, files, or witnesses that would be crucial to establish a breach or defenses (authorization, scope, exceptions)?
- For custody/support issues that are intertwined, what documents or evidence do we need to support our position on custody/support (income, parenting, school/medical records, witnesses)?
- Do we need expert evidence (e.g., forensic expert for electronic evidence, financial expert for damages/support calculations)?

C. Discovery & timing
- What discovery should we serve before the status conference (document requests, interrogatories, requests for admission, preservation letters, subpoenas)?
- Can we realistically obtain key records in time? If not, what priority items should we pursue first?
- Should we ask the court for expedited discovery or preservation orders now?
- Who should be deposed before the status conference (opposing party, custodial witnesses, expert witnesses)?

D. Preservation & spoliation
- Should we (or should you) send a formal litigation hold/preservation letter to the former business partner and any third-party custodians? If so, when?
- What specific devices/accounts must we preserve (phones, laptops, cloud drives, email, social media, business servers)?
- If we suspect evidence has been deleted or altered, what steps should we take immediately to preserve or recover it?

E. Admissibility / authentication
- Are there potential admissibility problems with any of our evidence (hearsay, authentication, privilege, chain of custody)?
- How should we document/authenticate electronic records (metadata, screenshots, original files, affidavits)?

F. Custody/support overlap
- Does the NDA dispute affect custody/support claims (or vice versa) in ways that change our evidence needs?
- Will discovery for the NDA case allow us to get relevant custody/support evidence, or do we need separate filings?
- Do we need to file any temporary custody/support motions now to protect the children before the status conference?

G. Remedies & settlement
- What remedies are realistically available for an NDA breach (injunction, damages, attorneys’ fees, punitive, liquidated)?
- Should we explore mediation or a settlement proposal before the status conference? If so, what would be our negotiating priorities?
- What are the risks and benefits of moving toward trial versus settlement now?

H. Costs, experts & logistics
- What would be the estimated cost and timeline to obtain a forensic image or an expert report?
- Who pays for third-party subpoenas or expert retention, and can we recover those costs later?
- What secure method should I use to deliver documents to you (portal, encrypted email, physical copy)?

I. Next steps for you (what you should do)
- What specific documents do you want me to gather and in what order of priority?
- Should I prepare a timeline/matrix of events and communications? If so, what format do you prefer?

Concrete documents and items to gather (prioritize highest-impact first)
- Copy of the NDA and any amendments, side agreements, or termination notices.
- All communications between you and the former partner (email chains, text messages, DMs, social media messages). Export originals if possible (not just screenshots).
- Any drafts of the disputed material (draft agreements, drafts of communications showing intent).
- Records showing use/disclosure of confidential info (published documents, distribution lists, third-party recipients).
- Business records: invoices, client lists, contracts, meeting minutes, internal memos showing alleged misuse.
- Financial records showing damages or gains (bank statements, invoices, tax returns, profit/loss statements).
- For custody/support: pay stubs, tax returns, child care receipts, school/medical records, calendars, parenting communications, police/protective orders if any.
- Witness contact info and short written summaries of what each witness can say.
- Device info: which phones/computers/accounts may contain evidence and who has access.

Immediate preservation steps (do these now)
- Stop deleting or altering any relevant files, messages, posts, or account settings.
- Back up your devices and relevant accounts (export emails, screenshot social media with timestamps, create copies of files).
- Make a simple timeline (date, short description, where the evidence is located).
- Tell any witnesses not to delete communications and ask them to preserve relevant records.
- Ask Robert to send a formal preservation/litigation-hold letter to the opposing party and relevant third parties immediately.

How to organize and deliver documents to your attorney
- Use clear folder names and dates (e.g., “NDA - Emails - 2024-03”).
- Keep original files where possible; include a short index or spreadsheet listing each file, date, source, and a one-line description.
- Provide native files (emails with headers, original PDFs, CSVs) when possible rather than screenshots.
- Use a secure method Robert prefers (encrypted upload, firm portal, secure USB hand-delivered).

What to expect at the status conference
- The judge usually addresses scheduling, discovery deadlines, motions, and whether the case is ready for settlement discussions or trial setting.
- Be ready to report on a discovery plan and what you have preserved and produced.
- The judge may set deadlines for expert disclosures, dispositive motions, and further hearings.

Suggested short questions/phrases you can say to Robert in the meeting or email
- “Can you review these communications and tell me which ones are most critical?”
- “Please send a preservation letter now — who should receive it?”
- “Which discovery requests should we serve immediately?”
- “Do you recommend a forensic image of my devices? If so, who will do it and when?”
- “What documents do you want me to prioritize sending to you this week?”
- “Do we need any temporary custody/support orders before the status conference?”

Offer to help
- I can draft a one-page evidence checklist or a short email to Robert Kim summarizing the items and asking these questions. Would you like me to prepare that now? If so, tell me whether you want a brief email or a longer checklist attachment and I’ll draft it for you.

