Good — this is a clear, manageable set of issues. Below I’ve organized practical, attorney-focused questions you can bring to Robert Kim, plus a concrete evidence checklist and a two-month action plan so you’ll be ready for the status conference. If you’d like, I can draft a short email to Robert pulling these items together.

Questions to ask your attorney (organized by topic)
- Case status and expectations for the status conference
  - What is likely to happen at the status conference? What will the judge expect us to report?
  - What procedural or scheduling orders should we be prepared to propose (discovery deadlines, motion cutoffs, trial date estimates)?
  - Will the court issue a case management order? If so, what deadlines are typical in Hennepin County civil division?

- Evidence and preservation
  - Do we need to issue a litigation hold or preservation notice now to preserve emails, phones, servers, and paper records?
  - Which documents and electronic records are most critical for proving the NDA claim and responding to their defenses?
  - How should we preserve and collect electronic evidence (metadata, original files, device forensics)? Do we need a forensic vendor?

- Discovery and timing
  - What discovery do you recommend initiating before the status conference (document requests, interrogatories, requests for admission, subpoenas)?
  - Are there imminent deadlines for serving discovery, or for responding to opposing party’s requests?
  - How long will third-party requests (banks, phone carriers, cloud providers) take — do we need to serve subpoenas now to get records in time?

- Witnesses and evidence authentication
  - Which witnesses should we identify and are any depositions advisable before the status conference?
  - Should we start collecting written witness statements or affidavits now?
  - How will we authenticate electronic communications (emails, texts, Slack)? What forms of proof does the court prefer?

- Experts and financial evidence
  - Do we need an expert (forensic accountant, forensic IT, valuation expert, damages expert) and what are the retention deadlines?
  - What financial documents will we need to quantify damages (invoices, bank records, tax returns, ledgers)?

- Custody and support overlap
  - Are the custody/support issues part of this civil case or are they in family court? If separate, how will they affect strategy here?
  - If they are relevant here, should we seek bifurcation or a protective order to limit disclosure of sensitive family material?
  - Do we need to obtain or provide financial documents relevant to support or custody (pay stubs, tax returns) and how will confidentiality be handled?

- Protective orders, confidentiality, and NDA issues
  - Should we seek a protective order or sealing order to protect confidential business information and personal/family information?
  - Given this is an NDA dispute, what steps protect trade secrets or confidential materials during discovery?

- Settlement and ADR
  - Is mediation or settlement negotiation realistic before or after the status conference?
  - Would an early neutral evaluation or mediator help narrow the issues, especially given the personal/business relationship with the opposing party?

- Strategy and burdens
  - What is our burden of proof on the NDA claim and what specific evidence will satisfy it?
  - What are the likely defenses and what evidence will they need to overcome them?

- Practical/logistical questions
  - What do you want me to bring to our next meeting (printouts, device access, summaries)?
  - How much time will you need to prepare materials for the status conference and what are the costs I should expect?
  - Can you prepare a short status memo for the court or should I draft an outline for you to finalize?

Concrete evidence checklist (start gathering these now)
- NDA and contractual documents
  - Signed NDA(s), amendments, or other related contracts
  - Draft versions of the NDA and related negotiations (tracked changes, emails)

- Communications
  - Emails, text messages, Slack/Teams messages, WhatsApp, social media messages between you and your former partner and relevant third parties
  - Meeting notes, calendar invites, call logs

- Business records & financials
  - Bank statements, ledgers, invoices, receipts, accounting spreadsheets
  - Tax returns, payroll records, expense reports
  - Profit/loss statements, balance sheets for relevant time frames

- Project/work evidence
  - Product files, code repositories, design files, versions with timestamps
  - Client lists, contracts with clients, invoices showing use or disclosure of information

- Evidence of harm/damages
  - Loss calculations, lost revenue evidence, proof of competitive harm
  - Marketing or sales documents showing impact

- Authentication & metadata
  - Original files where possible (not just screenshots), with metadata preserved
  - Device information and backups (phones, laptops); note when items were last accessed

- Witness materials
  - Names and contact info of potential witnesses
  - Short written summaries of each witness’s knowledge (timeline of what they saw/heard)

- Custody/support records (if relevant here or in related proceedings)
  - Court orders, parenting time logs, childcare receipts, school records
  - Pay stubs, W-2s, tax returns, benefits info, child-related expense receipts

Immediate actions to take (first 2–3 days)
- Don’t delete or alter any documents or messages related to the case. If you use email/cloud apps, stop deleting threads.
- Make backups of relevant devices and documents (local secure copy and an encrypted cloud copy).
- Create a simple chronology (date, event, source of evidence) — even a one-page timeline is very helpful.
- List names and contact info for potential witnesses.

Two-month planning timeline (high-level)
- Week 1: Contact Robert (or schedule meeting). Ask about litigation hold and immediate preservation steps. Provide initial chronology and top-priority documents.
- Weeks 1–3: Gather and organize documents listed above. Produce an exhibit folder or shared secure folder for your lawyer.
- Weeks 2–5: With your lawyer, identify discovery to serve (document requests, interrogatories, subpoenas). Decide whether to retain experts; if so, retain promptly.
- Weeks 4–6: Serve or respond to discovery as appropriate. Start securing third-party records (bank, phone, cloud) via subpoena if needed.
- Weeks 6–8: Prepare for status conference — draft a proposed discovery schedule and issues list for the court. Finalize witness/exhibit list and determine if any motions (protective order, sealing, emergency relief) should be filed before the conference.

What to bring to your meeting with Robert
- A one-page chronology of major events
- Copies (or access) to the NDA and any key contracts
- Samples of key communications (email threads, texts)
- List of witnesses and what they can testify to
- A list of assets, financial documents, and any evidence of harm

