Good — I can help you get organized. Below is a focused list of specific questions to bring to Robert Kim, plus a practical evidence checklist and short next-step plan so you can be ready well before the status conference in two months. I’m not giving legal advice; these are suggested questions and organizational steps to help your meeting with your attorney.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney
- Status conference logistics and goals
  - What is normally addressed at a civil-status conference in Hennepin County (what will the judge expect from us)?
  - What does the judge typically want us to have prepared (proposed scheduling order, discovery plan, statement of issues, settlement readiness)?
  - Do we need to file anything in advance of the status conference (joint status report, proposed order, initial disclosures)?
  - What timeline for discovery/trial should we propose, and can we realistically meet it?

- Evidence & discovery scope
  - Based on our NDA claim, what specific categories of evidence do you think are essential to prove breach and damages?
  - Which items must we produce or exchange before the status conference vs. can be obtained later?
  - Are there any documents or data we should prioritize obtaining now (emails, financials, access logs, client lists, prototypes, marketing materials)?
  - What discovery tools should we use first (requests for production, interrogatories, subpoenas to third parties, depositions)?

- Preservation / spoliation risk
  - Should we send a litigation hold notice now? What should it say and to whom?
  - Is there a risk the opposing party or third parties will delete/destroy evidence? If so, should we seek a temporary order to preserve evidence?
  - Do you recommend forensic imaging of devices or backups now? If so, how do we arrange that?

- Authentication & metadata
  - How will we authenticate emails, files, access logs, or other electronic evidence? What metadata should we preserve?
  - Do we need IT/forensic witnesses to verify authenticity or chain of custody?

- Witnesses & testimony
  - Which witnesses should we get statements from now? Which are high priority for early depositions?
  - Should we reach out to potential third-party witnesses (vendors, clients, former employees) now for affidavits or preservation?

- Experts & damages
  - Will we likely need expert witnesses (for damages, valuation, forensic IT, or industry practices)? If so, what deadlines apply for expert reports?
  - What documentary support do we need to back up damages claims (profit/loss, lost contracts, accounting records)?

- Custody/support intersection
  - How does the involvement of custody and support issues affect evidence disclosure or admissibility in the civil case?
  - Should anything involving children or sensitive family information be sealed or handled under protective order?
  - Might family-court matters be consolidated or communicated with the civil case? Will disclosure in civil litigation expose family-court records?
  - Are there strategy risks in introducing family-related evidence into this civil dispute?

- Protective orders & confidentiality
  - Given this is an NDA dispute, should we seek a protective order for confidential business/financial materials? When do we file it?
  - How can we avoid waiving privileges or confidentiality when producing documents?

- Immediate motions & tactical questions
  - Should we file any short-term motions before the status conference (e.g., motion to preserve, motion for temporary injunctive relief, motion to expedite discovery)?
  - If we think we need a preliminary injunction or TRO, what evidence/affidavit support is required and can we assemble it in time?

- Settlement & ADR
  - Is mediation or alternative dispute resolution appropriate before or after the status conference?
  - Should we prepare a settlement/position paper now (basis for settlement range, key non-negotiables)?

- Practical & procedural
  - What are the deadlines under Minnesota/Hennepin County rules for initial disclosures and for serving discovery?
  - How will we prioritize tasks across the next two months? What will you (Robert Kim) take care of and what do you need from me and by when?
  - What are the estimated costs and time commitments for doing the discovery we’ll need before the status conference?

Document & evidence checklist (start collecting and organizing these now)
- Contract documents
  - Original NDA and any amendments, attachments, or related agreements
  - Correspondence about the NDA negotiation/execution (emails, messages, text)

- Communications
  - All emails, texts, Slack/Teams messages, DMs between you and the former partner and relevant third parties
  - Any evidence the opposing party shared the confidential material (emails to third parties, public posting, client disclosures)

- Work products & data
  - Drafts and final versions of work the NDA covered (plans, proposals, product specs)
  - Access logs, file metadata, version histories, cloud storage audit logs

- Financial & damages evidence
  - Contracts and invoices showing lost business, revenue comparisons, accounting statements, profit/loss reports
  - Client lists, contact databases, and any evidence of client contact/loss after the alleged disclosure

- Device & third-party records
  - Phone records, device inventories, backups, server logs, forensic images (if available/appropriate)
  - Records from third parties (vendors, hosting providers) that could show disclosures or transfers

- Witness info
  - Names, contact info, and short summaries of what each witness can attest to
  - Any written statements or timelines from witnesses

- Custody/support related records (if relevant to evidence or damages)
  - Financial records used in support calculations (pay stubs, bank statements)
  - Communications that touch both business and family issues (be mindful of privacy concerns)
  - Any court orders or filings in family court that may intersect

How to organize what you collect
- Create a master index (spreadsheet) with: document ID, date, sender, recipient, short description, relevance, location (folder/file), and whether original is available
- Build a simple timeline of events (dates and short notes)
- Keep originals intact; make copies and store a copy set in a separate secure location
- Preserve electronic metadata (don’t convert to PDF without keeping originals) and document chain of custody
- Flag especially sensitive items for possible protective-order treatment

Priorities for the next 60 days (practical plan)
1. Immediately: litigation hold and stop any deletion of relevant records (ask Robert to draft/send).
2. Within 1–2 weeks: assemble the prioritized document categories above and the witness list; start the master index and timeline.
