Top questions to ask Robert Kim (high priority)
- What is the purpose of the status conference in the civil division (what will the judge decide/expect)?
- Based on our current pleadings, what elements of the NDA claim (and any defenses/counterclaims) do we need to prove/disprove?
- Do you think our existing evidence is enough to meet those elements and to support damages (if any)? If not, what’s missing?
- What specific documents or types of evidence would strengthen our case now? Which are most time-sensitive?
- Do you recommend immediate preservation steps (litigation hold letters, preserve ESI, collect phones/computers)?
- Should we plan to seek discovery (interrogatories, RFPs, subpoenas) before the status conference or after the court sets a schedule?
- Are depositions or expert reports likely to be necessary? If so, what kind and when should we retain experts (forensic accountant, e-discovery, industry expert)?
- How do the custody/support matters affect the civil case? Do we need to coordinate with family court or request protective orders to keep sensitive material confidential?
- Do we need any emergency relief or temporary orders before the status conference (e.g., injunction, seizure of documents)?
- What is the likely timeline from the status conference to discovery deadlines, motion deadlines, and trial? What deadlines should we be prepared to propose at the status conference?
- Should we prepare a proposed discovery plan or scheduling order to present at the status conference?
- Do you recommend mediation or other ADR before the next court date?
- What should I bring/submit to you before your review (format, index, bates numbering, how many copies)?
- What are estimated costs and likely next-stage expenses if we pursue full discovery or retain experts?
- Are there privilege concerns (attorney-client, work product) with documents I’ve saved? Do we need a privilege log?

Documents and evidence to gather (prioritize)
Immediate (collect and preserve now)
- Original NDA and any amendments, ancillary agreements (partnership agreements, buy-sell, employment agreements).
- All communications relating to the NDA: emails, text messages, Slack/Teams messages, DMs, voicemails—especially around the time of alleged breach(s).
- Drafts of the NDA or versions showing negotiation (if relevant).
- Documents showing alleged disclosure or use of confidential info (copies of the confidential material if available).
- Business records that show damages or misuse: contracts, customer lists, revenue reports, invoices, bank statements, payment records.
- Access logs, file access histories, server logs, version control/commit logs if materials are digital.
- Evidence of any third-party disclosures (who else received the material).
- Notes, calendar entries, meeting minutes, and witness contact info (names, roles, best contact).
- Any prior correspondence about resolving disputes, demands, or admissions.
- For custody/support interconnection: documents showing how the same facts overlap (do not file these publicly without checking confidentiality).

Follow-up (next 2–4 weeks)
- Tax returns, profit & loss statements, balance sheets if damages are financial.
- Draft witness statements or short affidavits summarizing key witness recollection.
- Copies of any public/online posts, social media, or website content relevant to the dispute.
- Any physical evidence or devices (USB drives, printed documents). Photograph or forensically image devices before altering them.

Witnesses and expert considerations
- Identify all witnesses: former partners, employees, clients, IT staff, anyone who saw or was told the information.
- Ask whether depositions should be scheduled soon (witness availability).
- Discuss potential experts: forensic IT (to prove access/transfer), forensic accountant (to calculate damages), industry expert (to show competitive harm).
- Consider whether witness affidavits can be prepared now to preserve testimony, especially for witnesses likely to be unavailable later.

Preservation & e-discovery steps to do now
- Issue litigation hold instructions for yourself and any relevant custodians (partners, IT, employees).
- Preserve phones/computers/servers; if devices are likely to be reviewed, consult counsel about forensic imaging.
- Save native files and metadata (don’t just save PDFs/screenshots if metadata matters).
- Back up relevant social media/accounts and export messages from platforms (Slack, WhatsApp).
- Tell third parties (cloud providers, accountants) to preserve records if they may be subpoenaed.

What to prepare for the status conference
- A short chronology (one-page timeline) of key dates/events related to the NDA and alleged breach.
- A one-page list of witnesses and brief summary of their expected testimony.
- A preliminary list of exhibits (documents you think are critical).
- A proposed scheduling plan to hand the court (recommended discovery timeline, cutoffs, mediation window).
- A list of pending discovery requests you plan to serve (if any) and dates.
- A note on any confidentiality concerns and request for a protective order if necessary.

Questions about coordination with custody/support issues
- Does civil court in Hennepin County need to coordinate with family court on overlapping discovery (custody/support)? Will sensitive family material be exposed?
- Should we file a motion to seal or request in-camera review for sensitive materials tied to custody/support?
- Could any disclosures in the civil case create risks in the custody/support case? How do we minimize that?

Practical organization and format tips to ask your lawyer
- How do you want documents sent (secure portal, encrypted email, PDF vs native)?
- Should I bates-stamp and index documents now or wait for your team to do it?
- Preferred file structure (chronology, exhibits folder, communications folder, financials).
- Ask for a short checklist of what Robert wants to review first.

Suggested immediate action items for you (timeline)
- Today–48 hours: Send Robert a short email requesting a meeting before the status conference and attach the one-page chronology and list of key documents/witnesses.
- Within 1–2 weeks: Issue preservation holds (with Robert’s guidance) and collect the immediate evidence list above.
- Within 3–4 weeks: Work with your lawyer to prioritize documents to produce vs. preserve behind privilege; start drafts of witness statements.
- 4–6 weeks before status conference: Decide on experts or propose depositions if necessary; prepare the proposed scheduling order and exhibit list.
- 1–2 weeks before status conference: Finalize what you will present at the status conference and confirm logistics (who will attend, filings required).

