Good — below is a focused, practical list you can use with Robert Kim to make sure you’re ready for the status conference in two months. I’ve grouped items so you can run through them quickly in a call or email, and included a short prioritized action checklist you can start on right away.

Suggested questions to ask your attorney
- Case status & judge’s expectations
  - What specifically will the judge want to address at the status conference? (case schedule, discovery deadlines, settlement prospects, motions, custody issues, etc.)
  - Are there any mandatory filings or reports we must submit before the conference?
  - What outcome should we aim for at the status conference?

- Evidence & admissibility for the NDA dispute
  - Based on the NDA claim, what specific evidence do we need to prove breach and damages? (e.g., signed NDA, proof information was disclosed, evidence of confidential status, proof of harm or benefit)
  - Are draft versions of the NDA, email chains discussing confidentiality, or internal documents useful? How should they be preserved and organized?
  - Will emails, text messages, screenshots, or social media posts be admissible — what form of authentication is required?
  - Do we need forensic copies of devices or server logs (to prove access/disclosure)? If so, when should we get that done and by whom?
  - Should we get an expert (forensics, valuation of damages, economics) now or wait until after initial discovery?
  - If the opposing party used or profited from the information, what evidence do we need to show causation and measure damages?

- Discovery, preservation, and spoliation
  - What immediate steps should I take to preserve evidence (devices, accounts, documents)? (e.g., avoid deleting messages, stop automatic deletion, preserve originals)
  - Should we send a preservation/preservation-letter to the opposing party or third parties?
  - Do we need subpoenas to third parties (banks, cloud providers, email hosts)? When should those be issued?
  - What discovery should we propound now (interrogatories, requests for production, admissions)? What’s the timing?

- Custody & support overlap
  - How do the custody/support issues affect our civil NDA case (e.g., discovery scope, confidentiality, scheduling)?
  - What financial documents are needed for support issues (tax returns, pay stubs, bank statements, benefits, expense records)?
  - Should custody evaluations or parenting-time evidence be obtained before the status conference?
  - Will issues in family law court complicate discovery or protective orders in the civil case?

- Strategy, settlement, and timelines
  - What is our litigation strategy going into the status conference? (narrow issues, ask for scheduling order, mediation, early motion)
  - Are we likely to need depositions before the next conference? If so, whom and what timeline?
  - Is mediation or settlement realistic before the conference? If yes, what information or documents would help settlement talks?
  - What are realistic deadlines for discovery, motions, and expert reports given the conference date?

- Practical & cost questions
  - What will be the approximate costs and timing for key tasks (forensic imaging, expert reports, subpoenas, depositions)?
  - Should we prioritize certain evidence now based on cost-benefit?
  - Any protective order or confidentiality procedures we should seek to protect sensitive materials?

- Administrative/logistical
  - Do you want a one-page timeline of events and a folder of key documents prepared in advance? If so, what format is best for you?
  - How should I label or organize documents (e.g., Bates numbering, exhibit list)?
  - Are there any upcoming deadlines I need to calendar immediately?

Evidence checklist — items to collect/organize now
- Core documents
  - Signed NDA and any amendments or drafts
  - Communications about the NDA (emails, texts, messaging app logs, meeting notes)
  - Copies of the allegedly disclosed confidential information (documents, files, images)
  - Any written warnings, confidentiality notices, or internal protection measures you used

- Proof of disclosure and dissemination
  - Emails, messages, or witness statements showing the opposing party shared or used the information
  - Access logs, server logs, downloads, or timestamps showing who accessed files
  - Screenshots (with metadata preserved) and originals where possible

- Proof of damages or benefit
  - Financial records showing lost revenue, business opportunity details, or profits attributable to the disclosure
  - Contracts lost or changed because of the disclosure
  - Expert analyses or valuations (if already available, or note you may need one)

- For custody/support intersection
  - Tax returns (last 2–3 years), W-2/1099, pay stubs, bank statements
  - Child care, medical, education expenses, and receipts
  - Parenting-time records, communications about custody, child schedules
  - Any prior court orders or custody agreements

- Witnesses & declarations
  - Names and contact info for witnesses who saw or heard disclosures or can testify to damages
  - Short written summaries of what each witness would testify to
  - Consider drafting declarations from witnesses you control (signed and dated)

How to organize and present evidence for your attorney
- Make a one-page timeline of key events (dates, short description, documents tied to each date).
- Create a master folder (digital PDF or physical) with folders: NDA & drafts, communications, disclosure proof, financials, witnesses, custody/support docs.
- Label items with a simple exhibit number (Exhibit 1, 2, 3) and include a short index.
- Preserve originals and create read-only copies (PDFs with metadata when possible).
- If you provide electronic messages, preserve metadata and note the device/source.

Prioritized immediate actions (first 1–2 weeks)
1. Preserve devices/accounts: stop deleting messages, make copies if possible, and ask your lawyer before altering anything.
2. Gather and scan core docs: NDA, all communications, key financial statements, and custody documents.
3. Make a one-page timeline and list of witnesses for Robert Kim.
4. Ask Robert about immediate need for forensic imaging or a preservation letter to the opposing party.

Tasks to aim to complete within 2–6 weeks
- Meet with Robert to finalize discovery requests and decide on subpoenas or experts.
- Serve or prepare written discovery (interrogatories, RFPs) if appropriate.
- Obtain forensic images or expert valuations as recommended.
- Prepare draft witness declarations or affidavits.

At/just before the status conference
- Bring (or file, if required) the one-page timeline, list of completed discovery, outstanding items, and proposed schedule.
- Be ready to propose a timeline for depositions, motions, expert reports, and trial or mediation dates.

Suggested concise questions you can send to Robert right away
- “What does the judge expect at the status conference, and are there any filings we must make before then?”
- “Based on our NDA claim, which specific documents and types of proof should I prioritize collecting now?”
- “Should we preserve/forensically image devices and issue preservation letters or subpoenas now?”
- “How do custody/support issues change our discovery needs or strategy?”
- “Do you recommend an expert at this stage? What are the expected costs and timing?”

Would you like me to:
- Draft a short email to Robert Kim with these questions and your timeline, or
- Create a one-page timeline template you can fill in and print?

If you want, tell me how much you already have collected (e.g., NDA copy, emails, bank statements), and I’ll help you prioritize exactly what to produce first.

