Note: this is organizational help — not legal advice. Follow Robert’s legal guidance for strategy and filings.

A. Top questions to ask Robert (prioritized)
- Preservation and spoliation
  - Do we need to send litigation‑hold/preservation notices now? To whom (employees, vendors, ex‑partner)?
  - What specific devices/accounts must be preserved (phones, laptops, servers, cloud storage, email, backups)?
- Evidence needs and priorities
  - What evidence do you consider most important to prove breach of the NDA and the elements of damages?
  - Which documents or ESI should I gather first (emails, contracts, bank records, access logs, drafts)?
  - Do we need a forensic IT expert to collect metadata or make forensic images? If so, when should we retain one?
- Discovery plan and timing
  - What discovery do you want served before the status conference (interrogatories, RFPs, requests for admission, subpoenas)?
  - Should we seek expedited discovery or any protective orders for confidential materials?
  - What is the realistic timeline to complete initial discovery before the next status conference?
- Motions and emergency relief
  - Should we consider any pre‑conference motions (motion for protective order, motion to compel, or emergency injunction to stop disclosure/use of confidential info)?
- Coordination with custody/support issues
  - How do the custody/support matters affect this civil NDA matter? Should we coordinate filings with family court or keep them separate?
  - Are there any confidentiality or evidence rules in family court that affect what we produce here?
- Witnesses and testimony
  - Which witnesses should we identify or depose early (employees, accountants, IT administrators, clients)?
  - Do we need written statements or declarations from witnesses before the conference?
- Costs and strategy
  - What’s the estimated cost to collect/forensically preserve ESI and to proceed with initial discovery?
  - What are our settlement/ADR options and the timing for pursuing mediation before the status conference?
- Practicalities for the status conference
  - What specific orders or schedule do you intend to ask the judge for at the status conference?
  - What should I bring to the status conference (exhibits, a witness list, chronology)?
- Communication with opposing party
  - Should I communicate at all with my former partner before the conference? If so, what is safe to say?
- Confidentiality of evidence
  - How will confidential business information be protected (stipulated protective order, sealing, in‑camera submission)?

B. Evidence checklist — what to gather and preserve now
Documents and contracts
- Signed NDA and any amendments, side letters, or related agreements
- Contracts between you and the former partner or third parties that reference confidential materials
Communications (emails, texts, DMs)
- All emails between you and the partner (including attachments)
- Text messages, iMessage, WhatsApp, Signal, Slack, social media DMs
- Drafts of documents or proposals showing development of confidential info
Work product / business records
- Financial records showing damages or benefit to the other side: bank statements, invoices, client lists, sales records, accounting files, tax returns (relevant portions)
- Access logs, server or cloud activity logs, file sharing/download logs, backups, version history
- Internal notes, meeting minutes, calendars, project plans, task lists
Digital evidence / metadata
- Native files (not just PDFs/screenshots) with metadata
- File modification and creation timestamps, file access logs
- Forensic images of devices when appropriate
Witness materials
- Names and contact info for employees, contractors, clients who can corroborate misuse or confidentiality
- Written statements or timelines from key witnesses (if available)
Other
- Marketing materials or competitive documents that the former partner used/published
- Any public postings or social media that reveal the disputed information
- Court filings or family court documents related to custody/support that may overlap

C. Discovery & litigation actions to discuss with Robert
- Immediate preservation: litigation‑hold letter, forensic imaging for critical devices.
- Draft and serve targeted discovery (RFPs, interrogatories) tailored to prove (1) the covered confidential information, (2) how the partner obtained/used it, and (3) damages/benefit.
- ESI protocol: agree on formats, search terms, custodians, and rolling production schedule.
- Protective order: request confidentiality protections for business/child‑related sensitive materials.
- Subpoenas: third‑party financial institutions, cloud providers, or hosting companies (plan lead time).
- Expert retention: forensic IT expert, damages or accounting expert — timing and scope.
- Depositions: whom to depose and desired timeline before next scheduling hearing.
- Settlement/ADR: timing for mediation or early neutral evaluation, and whether to propose ADR at the status conference.
- Anticipate opposing party’s likely defenses/evidence and ask Robert what discovery can target those issues.

D. Two‑month prep timeline (suggested)
Weeks 1–2 (immediate)
- Ask Robert to issue litigation‑hold notices and give written instructions on preservation.
- Gather and upload to a secure folder all NDA, contracts, emails, texts, financial records, and key documents listed above.
- Make an inventory (spreadsheet) of documents/devices and custodians.
- Discuss and decide whether to retain a forensic expert.
Weeks 3–4
- Work with Robert to finalize and serve initial discovery (targeted RFPs/interrogatories/requests for admission).
- Begin collecting third‑party records that don’t require subpoenas.
- Start drafting witness lists and short witness statements/timelines.
Weeks 5–6
- Review responses (if any) and meet with Robert to assess gaps.
- If needed, prepare motions (motion to compel or motion for protective order) or notices to third parties.
- Confirm experts and scope of their work so they can begin analysis.
Weeks 7–8 (final prep)
- Prepare a short exhibit binder/chronology to bring to the status conference.
- Coordinate with Robert on what to say at the conference and the scheduling proposal (deadlines, depositions, expert reports, mediation).
- Final check on outstanding subpoenas/evidence and identify what must be prioritized after the conference.

Practical tips for meeting/communicating with Robert
- Bring a one‑page chronology of events and a prioritized list of the 10 most important documents.
- Provide access (secure link) to a folder with organized subfolders: Contracts, Communications, Financials, Digital Evidence, Witnesses, Family‑related docs.
- Ask Robert for a short written memo of action items after your meeting so you can track deadlines.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise email to Robert with these questions and your preferred meeting times.
- Convert the evidence checklist into a one‑page inventory template or spreadsheet you can use to track documents and custodians.
- Help you draft a short chronology of events to bring to your attorney.

