Good — I’ll give you a focused, practical checklist and a set of specific questions you can bring to or send Robert Kim so you’re fully prepared for the status conference in two months. I’ll also include a short preservation/collection plan and a suggested script/email you can use to contact him.

Quick summary to frame the questions
- Case: NDA dispute (civil division, Hennepin County). Opposing party: former business partner.
- Related family issues: custody and support intertwined (may affect evidence and strategy).
- Next milestone: status conference in ~2 months.
Goal: identify what additional evidence you should collect before that status conference and what your lawyer should do in the meantime.

Immediate checklist (what to do now — within 1 week)
1. Preservation (high priority)
   - Ask your attorney to send a litigation hold / preservation letter to the opposing party and their counsel.
   - Preserve all devices, accounts, servers, backups, cloud storage, and paper files that may contain relevant information. Do not delete messages or files.
   - Take images/screenshots of any time-sensitive web pages, posts, or messages (include visible timestamps).

2. Gather and secure key documents (start collecting now)
   - The NDA(s): signed copies, amendments, drafts, and any communications about them.
   - Partnership / business agreements, ownership documentation, meeting minutes.
   - Communications with the former partner: emails, text messages, DMs, Slack/Teams, notes from phone calls, voicemails.
   - Financial records: bank statements, invoices, receipts, tax returns, payment records showing relevant transfers or revenue.
   - Contracts, client lists, sales records, marketing materials, product development notes that relate to alleged breach/damages.
   - Any documents showing your mitigation efforts or attempts to resolve the dispute.

3. Custody/support-related records
   - Court orders (existing family court orders), child support payment records, bank transfers.
   - Communications regarding custody, parenting plans, exchanges, school/medical records that bear on custody/support.
   - Records of expenses for the children, daycare, extracurriculars, etc.

4. Witness list
   - Make a list (with contact info) of people who have direct knowledge: employees, clients, accountants, IT admins, witnesses to communications.

5. Timeline
   - Draft a concise, chronological timeline of events (key dates, actions, communications, alleged breaches). This is one of the most useful things you can prepare.

What to accomplish in the next 2–6 weeks
- Work with Robert to decide which discovery to serve early: preservation demand, written discovery (RFPs, interrogatories), and initial document requests.
- Decide whether to request emergency/temporary relief (if there’s ongoing harm from alleged disclosure).
- Consider whether expert retention is necessary (forensic IT, forensic accountant, valuation expert, or custody evaluator).
- If ESI will be important, coordinate list of accounts, devices, and any needed forensic imaging.
- Discuss whether to file any pre-conference motions (motion for protective order, motion to compel preservation, motion for temporary restraining order, etc.).
- Prepare any required status conference submissions (proposed case management order, joint status report, list of issues).

Suggested categories of evidence to collect (with examples)
- NDA evidence: signed NDAs, amendments, proof of receipt, witness testimony about signing/notice.
- Breach evidence: copies/screenshots of the disclosed content, server logs, downloads, publication timestamps, third-party use.
- Damages evidence: lost contracts, cancelled deals, revenue loss, forecasts, accounting records.
- Chain-of-custody / provenance: metadata, email headers, server logs, IP logs, device images.
- Communications evidence: preserved texts, emails, social media messages, collaboration platform messages.
- Custody/support evidence: court filings, payment records, school records, communications showing parenting/behavioral issues.
- Third-party records: client contracts, invoices, vendor records, bank transfers, payroll records.

Targeted questions to ask Robert Kim (use these in your meeting or email)
Procedural / timeline
1. What exactly will happen at the status conference and what does the judge expect us to present?
2. What filings (if any) should we submit before the status conference (joint status report, proposed case schedule, list of disputed issues)?
3. Are there any imminent deadlines (disclosures, local rule requirements) we must meet before then?
4. Will the court set a discovery schedule at that conference? If so, how long will we have for fact discovery, expert disclosures, and dispositive motions?

Preservation & discovery
5. Should you send a formal litigation hold/preservation letter now? Will you send it or should I?
6. What specific documents and devices should I preserve immediately?
7. Which discovery tools do you recommend serving first (RFPs, interrogatories, requests for admission, subpoenas, depositions)?
8. Should we subpoena third parties (banks, hosting providers, IT vendors)? If yes, who specifically?

Evidence & collection
9. Do we have enough evidence now to show an actual or threatened disclosure? What’s missing?
10. What additional documents or metadata would be most persuasive on the NDA breach and damages?
11. For electronic evidence, should we get a forensic image of devices or server logs? Who would perform that?
12. What steps can I take to preserve metadata and chain of custody (naming, hashing, forensic copy)?
13. Do we need to get accountant or valuation documents now to quantify damages, or is that for later?

Witnesses & experts
14. Which witnesses should I line up now? Do we need written witness statements or affidavits before the conference?
15. Should we retain an expert now (forensic IT, accountant, valuation expert, child custody evaluator)? If so, who do you recommend and when should they be engaged?
16. For custody/support issues, would a guardian ad litem or evaluator be helpful at this stage?

Family law intersection
17. How will the custody/support matters affect strategy in the civil case (timing, evidence sharing, confidentiality)?
18. Do we need to coordinate with family-law counsel? Should they be looped in now?
19. Could evidence from the civil case be used in family proceedings (or vice versa)? How do we protect privacy for the children?

Strategy & risk
20. Are we in a strong position to seek injunctive relief or an interim order (e.g., stop use/distribution of disclosed material)?
21. What are the realistic settlement options and timeline? Should we prepare a settlement demand or confidentiality agreement now?
22. What are the main weaknesses in our evidence and how can they be addressed before the conference?

Protective order & confidentiality
23. Do we need to request a protective order early (so sensitive documents can be produced without risk)?
24. How do we handle privileged documents or communications that might be relevant?

Logistics & costs
25. What is the estimated cost for the next 2 months of discovery (forensic imaging, expert fees, depositions)?
26. Which tasks can I do personally (collecting documents) vs. those that require counsel or experts?

Status conference preparation
27. What should I prepare to present at the status conference (timeline, witness list, exhibit list)?
28. Do you want me to draft a short summary/timeline for you to submit?
29. Will you want a pre-conference meeting to sign off on strategy and documents?

Practical/administrative
30. How should I organize and label the evidence (naming conventions, folders, file types) to make it easy for you?
31. Who should be our point person for obtaining business records or contacting witnesses?

Suggested immediate actions you can take (practical and low-cost)
- Create a single folder (digital + backup) labeled “NDA litigation — [case number/name]” and subfolders: NDA, Communications, Financials, Timeline, Custody, Witnesses, ESI images.
- Start a simple spreadsheet for the timeline and for witness/contact info.
- Export/save all relevant emails as PDFs with headers (showing dates and metadata).
- Save text messages and platform messages as screenshots and export if possible (include timestamps).
- Collect bank statements, invoices, tax returns relevant to damages and support.
- Make a short written chronology (1–2 pages) summarizing the dispute and key dates.

